Retire superseded SingleValue blobs at commit, using the existing durable deletion record.

Only incoming blob puts look up the key's visible predecessor. The lookup reads entry types
and blob sequence numbers and never reads or decompresses values. A lookup error is logged
and skips retirement for that key; the commit still proceeds.

The normal small and medium put path, merge behavior and on-disk format are unchanged.
Missing blob files are tolerated only during cleanup.

Not reclaimed by this patch: a blob overwritten by a non-blob (small, medium or inline)
value, explicit deletes, MultiValue blobs, and blobs orphaned before the patch. Those keep
relying on compaction.

A read-only process that opened the database before a commit can fail to read an overwritten
huge key right after that commit, not only after a compaction. Each commit that replaces a
blob writes one .del file; these accumulate until the next open, as compaction deletes do
today. On Windows, a failed unlink falls back to the existing deferred retry.

